-- title:
--   Alternating pair tensors along the width-two band path
-- statement:
--   For $n\in\mathbb{N}$ write $[n]=\{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$ and let $S_n$ be the symmetric group on $[n]$. A *word* of length $n$ over the alphabet $[d]$ is a map $a:[n]\to[d]$, and $W_{n,d}=\mathbb{C}^{[d]^n}$ is the space of complex functions on words, with standard basis vectors $e_a$ (the indicator function of the word $a$), so that $W_{n,d}\cong(\mathbb{C}^d)^{\otimes n}$. The group $S_n$ acts on $W_{n,d}$ by permuting positions, $(\rho_{n,d}(g)x)(a)=x(a\circ g)$; equivalently $\rho_{n,d}(g)e_a=e_{a\circ g^{-1}}$. For a subgroup $G\le S_n$ and a word $a$, $\operatorname{alt}_G(a)=\sum_{g\in G}\operatorname{sgn}(g)\,\rho_{n,d}(g)\,e_a$ is the signed alternation of $e_a$ over $G$, and for a map $f$ from $[n]$ to any set, $\operatorname{Fib}(f)=\{g\in S_n: f\circ g=f\}$ is its fiber group.
--
--   The staircase diagram $\delta_m=\{(i,j)\in\mathbb{N}^2:i+j<m\}$ has $m$ rows; for $h\in\mathbb{N}$ its width-two outer band in $\delta_{h+2}$ consists of the $2h+3$ cells with $h\le i+j<h+2$, listed along the lattice path $k\mapsto\big(h+1-\lfloor (k+1)/2\rfloor,\ \lfloor k/2\rfloor\big)$, $k=0,\dots,2h+2$, from $(h+1,0)$ to $(0,h+1)$; these band cells are also enumerated by band positions $[b]$, $b=2h+3$, via a fixed bijection. The file introduces the following objects; in items 1 to 3, $r\in\mathbb{N}$ is a parameter, positions are $\{0,\dots,2r\}$ and words use the alphabet $\{0,1\}$.
--
--   1. **Pair labels and base word.** On positions $\{0,\dots,2r\}$, the label $\operatorname{tag}(k)=\lfloor (k+1)/2\rfloor$, which takes the values $0,1,1,2,2,\dots,r,r$ (so the label classes are $\{0\},\{1,2\},\dots,\{2r-1,2r\}$), and the base word $\mathrm{b}_r$ with $\mathrm{b}_r(0)=0$ and $\mathrm{b}_r(k)=(k+1)\bmod 2$ for $k\ge1$, i.e. $0,0,1,0,1,\dots,0,1$.
--   2. **Alternating pair tensor.** $\alpha_r=\operatorname{alt}_{\operatorname{Fib}(\operatorname{tag})}(\mathrm{b}_r)\in W_{2r+1,2}$. The group $\operatorname{Fib}(\operatorname{tag})$ is generated by the transpositions of the pairs $\{2k-1,2k\}$, so explicitly
--   $$
--   \alpha_r=e_0\otimes\big(e_0\otimes e_1-e_1\otimes e_0\big)^{\otimes r},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \alpha_r(w)=[w_0=0]\prod_{k=0}^{r-1}J(w_{2k+1},w_{2k+2}),
--   $$
--   where $[\cdot]$ is the Iverson bracket and $J$ is the matrix of item 4.
--   3. **Appending a pair.** The bijection $\{0,\dots,2r+2\}\to[2r+1]\sqcup[2]$ sending the first $2r+1$ positions to the left summand and the positions $2r+1,2r+2$ to the right summand.
--   4. **The matrix $J$.**
--   $$
--   J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad\text{rows and columns indexed by }\{0,1\}.
--   $$
--   5. **Relabeling fiber groups.** For a bijection $\varphi:[n]\to[m]$ and a labeling $c$ of $[m]$, the bijection $\operatorname{Fib}(c\circ\varphi)\to\operatorname{Fib}(c)$, $g\mapsto\varphi\circ g\circ\varphi^{-1}$.
--   6. **Low letters.** The inclusion $\{0,1\}\hookrightarrow[h+2]$ of the two smallest letters into the alphabet $[h+2]$ of row indices of $\delta_{h+2}$.
--   7. **Reversed band path.** The bijection from $\{0,\dots,2h+2\}$ to the band positions that first reverses the order, $k\mapsto 2h+2-k$, and then follows the lattice path above; it traverses the band from $(0,h+1)$ to $(h+1,0)$.
--
--   In the surrounding development these objects identify the band factors that arise when $\delta_{h+2}$ is cut into $\delta_h$ and its width-two band. These factors are the row band word and the column band word: signed alternations, over the permutations of band positions preserving the row (respectively the column) of every band cell, of the band part of a fixed word recording rotated cell coordinates. The row band word, evaluated on words that use only the letters $0,1$ and read along the band path, equals $\alpha_{h+1}$, and the column band word read along the reversed path equals $\alpha_{h+1}$ as well. This turns the band contribution into an explicit tensor of $2\times2$ alternating blocks.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Items 1 to 4 live in the namespace `Saxl.PathLayer` as `tag`, `base`, `rowAlt`, `appendPair` (with the simp lemmas `appendPair_left` and `appendPair_right`) and `J`; items 5 to 7 are `fiberRelabel`, `lowLetter` and `bandColumnPathEquiv` (which composes `Fin.revPerm` with the path enumeration `bandPathEquiv`). The explicit product formula in item 2 is a property of $\alpha_r$ proved by a separate lemma, not part of its definition.
